<header><p class="enc-kicker">Religion / idea · deity</p><h1 class="enc-title">Athtar</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as 'Athtar, Attar</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Athtar (ʿAthtar) was a god of ancient <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-arabia">South Arabia</a> identified with the planet Venus, the male counterpart of the Mesopotamian Ishtar and the Levantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/astarte">Astarte</a>. In Sabaean and other South Arabian inscriptions he is often named first among the gods, and he was associated with rain and irrigation. His cult faded with the spread of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/monotheism">monotheism</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/yemen">Yemen</a> from the fourth century CE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/athtar.jpg" alt="Athtar" width="320" height="214" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anonymous (South Arabia)Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:South_Arabian_-_Fragment_of_a_Frieze_with_an_Ibex_and_Oryxes_-_Walters_2138.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
